<!-- §01 — THE SAFETY-SPEED TRAP -->
<section id="s-trap" data-code="trap">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Safety-Speed Trap</div>
<h2>Fast or safe — pick one?</h2>

<p>Speculative decoding accelerates LLM inference by having a small draft model propose candidate segments and the large target model verify them in parallel — cutting per-token latency by 2–3× in practice. But that draft-verify loop was designed for speed, not safety. The target model stamps its approval on semantic quality alone; it has no concept of whether a segment is a jailbreak.</p>

<p>Naively bolting on existing safety defenses makes things worse. Prompt-level methods (Self-Reminder, RPO) leave attack success rates above 30%. Decoding-time methods (SafeDecoding, SecDecoding) require parallel inference or token-level steering that disrupts the draft-verify alignment — and they actually <em>slow</em> the model below the un-accelerated baseline: SecDecoding runs at 0.61× the no-speculative speed on Qwen3-32B.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Safety-Speed Pareto Frontier — Qwen3-32B</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Hover a dot to identify the method</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-height:280px">
    <canvas id="pareto-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;cursor:crosshair"></canvas>
    <div id="pareto-tooltip" style="display:none;position:absolute;pointer-events:none;background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:.4rem .7rem;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--text2);z-index:10"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The scatter shows the fundamental incompatibility: methods in the top-right are fast but dangerous; methods in the bottom-left are safer but slow. SafeSpec (purple star) is the only method in the <strong>bottom-right</strong> — fast <em>and</em> safe.</p>

<p>The insight SafeSpec exploits is that the target model's verification pass already processes the full hidden-state sequence. Attaching a tiny MLP on top of that existing computation costs almost nothing — and gives you a safety signal for free.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 — SPECULATIVE DECODING PRIMER -->
<section id="s-spec" data-code="spec">
<div class="section-no">02 — Background</div>
<h2>How speculative decoding works</h2>

<p>Classic auto-regressive decoding generates one token per forward pass of the large model. Speculative decoding replaces this with a two-model loop: a small, fast <strong>draft model</strong> proposes a block of tokens (or a reasoning segment), and the large <strong>target model</strong> verifies the whole block in a single parallel forward pass. Accepted tokens are kept; rejected tokens cause the generation to revert to the rejection point.</p>

<p>SafeSpec builds on <strong>Specreason</strong> (Pan et al., 2025), which operates at segment granularity (a full reasoning step) rather than individual tokens. The target model scores each segment's quality by extracting the logit for digit tokens <code>0</code>–<code>9</code> — whichever digit has the highest logit becomes the integer quality score S<sub>q</sub>. Segments with S<sub>q</sub> ≥ τ<sub>q</sub> are accepted; otherwise the target model regenerates.</p>

<div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.75rem 0">
<svg id="spec-svg" viewBox="0 0 740 200" style="width:100%;min-width:580px;font-family:var(--ff-mono)" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
