<!-- §03 — THE PROBABILISTIC INSIGHT -->
<section id="s-insight" data-code="insight">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Probabilistic Insight</div>
<h2>Jailbreaks don't eliminate safe outputs — they hide them</h2>

<p>A jailbreak prompt doesn't magically rewrite the model's weights. It shifts the probability distribution over continuations — concentrating mass on harmful trajectories while suppressing, but not eliminating, the safe ones. Empirically, safe continuations still exist in the low-probability tail even for successful attacks.</p>

<p>This means the problem isn't binary. Instead of asking "is this generation safe or not?" and hard-blocking on failure, SafeSpec asks: <em>given that a single draw was unsafe, how many draws K do I need before finding at least one safe continuation?</em></p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Recovery probability</div>
<span class="math-var">p_safe</span> = Pr(single sample is safe)
<br>
<span class="math-var">P(K)</span> = 1 − (1 − <span class="math-var">p_safe</span>)<span style="vertical-align:super;font-size:.65em">K</span>
<br>
<span class="math-comment"># As long as p_safe &gt; 0, increasing K strictly raises recovery probability</span>
</div>

<p>The reflection prompt is the key multiplier: without it, the model's context is still "polluted" by the jailbreak framing, so p<sub>safe</sub> stays low even after rollback. Inserting an explicit safety-re-evaluation prompt forces the model to re-evaluate its intent and reshapes the distribution — raising p<sub>safe</sub> dramatically.</p>

