<!-- §05 — ROLLBACK AND REFLECT -->
<section id="s-reflect" data-code="reflect">
<div class="section-no">05 — Recovery Mechanism</div>
<h2>Rollback → Reflect → Resample</h2>

<p>Hard refusal has a hidden problem: the safety classifier has a non-trivial false positive rate. If you terminate generation every time the head fires, you'll refuse a shocking 73% of benign queries (see ablation table). SafeSpec instead treats an unsafe detection as a recoverable state, not a terminal failure.</p>

<p><strong>Step 1 — Rollback:</strong> Revert the generation context to the previous safe segment x<sub>n-1</sub>. The current state at x<sub>n</sub> is already corrupted by adversarial framing — rolling back moves the model to a high-probability safe region where p<sub>safe</sub> is naturally higher.</p>

<p><strong>Step 2 — Reflect:</strong> Insert a reflection prompt that explicitly asks the model to re-evaluate its intent. This isn't just cosmetic — it disrupts the narrative coherence that jailbreaks rely on, forcing the model to "zoom out" from the deceptive framing. Probabilistically, it reshapes the output distribution to concentrate mass on safe trajectories.</p>

<p><strong>Step 3 — Resample K candidates:</strong> Switch the draft model to multi-sampling: generate K independent candidate continuations. Each is re-verified by the safety head. Select the candidate with the highest safety score as the next segment.</p>

